  "variation": {
    "seeded": true,
    "notes": "Where each boulder starts, how it tumbles, its size and where it lands vary per cast. The warning time, the first landing and the last big boulder stay fixed so the danger is fair to read."
  },
  "director_notes": "An original rock slide from above (a genre move). Built from rock-look sparks: boulders are sparks with look rock and a big size_px, born over the target area in the sky (anchor sky, emit_radius_px) and dropped by gravity, so they tumble, land and lie as rubble. Timing: 0.55 s of warning (rumble, grit trickling down, a dark patch spreading on the ground as the telegraph), then boulders arrive in a staggered stream for about 0.4 s (emit_ms) so the crashes come one after another, then a beat later one huge boulder lands on top for the climax with a boom, the biggest shake and a crater. Sound: rumble -> crash every 110 ms -> boom + crash -> quiet rumble. The fall height is the template's space.scale (4 character heights); raise it for a longer, scarier fall."
}
